# Target C-II — near-simple signed composition of the degree/phase-gate theorem

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Branch theorem:** `research/target_c_global_straight_degree_phase_gate_2026-09-15.md`  
**Additional proved inputs:** `f4229fb4` / `64535791` (near-simple outer walk) and `2764fb64` / `6db7e252` (signed penetration injectivity and hostile audit)  
**Status:** proved proof-only composition; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN`

Two results landed after the degree/phase-gate theorem was started. They remove most
of its remaining topological loss and strengthen its transverse-edge conclusion.

1. The low-detour outer walk is almost simple: at the current endpoint the total
   repetition surplus of the outer facial walk is at most
   `0.0459365399111 D+o(D)`.
2. Every extra contact at a straight occurrence has a signed inward component, and
   the near-disk geometry prevents one physical contact from being used inward at both
   endpoints.

Composed with the `15`-degree degree-collapse theorem, these facts give

\[
\boxed{
V_{simp,15}\ge1.10837515220613\,D-o(D)
}
\]

distinct simple-outer unloaded degree-three/four straight vertices, and at least

\[
\boxed{
E_{all,15}\ge1.15431169211717\,D-o(D)
}
\]
ndistinct physical contacts with signed inward component greater than `1/sqrt(2)`.
The second bound uses **all** extra contacts, not one representative per straight
vertex.

---

## 1. Inputs at the current low-`t` endpoint

Set

\[
\beta={\pi\over12}.
\]

The degree/phase-gate theorem proves

\[
\boxed{
S_{15}/D
\ge1.1543116921171727469\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{CP.1}
\]

where `S_15` counts distinct physical vertices supporting a `15`-degree-straight
internal distinguished-route occurrence. Every such vertex is unloaded and has contact
degree exactly three or four.

The near-simple outer-walk theorem proves at the same endpoint

\[
\boxed{
{b-V_{out}\over D}
\le0.0459365399110338116\ldots+o(1),
}
\tag{CP.2}
\]

where `b` is outer-walk occurrence length and `V_out` is the number of distinct
physical outer-face vertices.

For the occurrence multiplicities `m_x>=1` of physical outer vertices,

\[
b-V_{out}=\sum_x(m_x-1).
\tag{CP.3}
\]

Thus (CP.2) is an exact budget for all repeated physical outer vertices, regardless of
whether their repeated occurrences are straight.

---

## 2. The repeated-outer branch is small

Let `A_15` be the number of physical vertices counted by `S_15` whose straight sector
is not their only outer-face incidence. Every such vertex has `m_x>=2` and contributes
at least one to the sum in (CP.3). Therefore

\[
\boxed{A_{15}\le b-V_{out}.}
\tag{CP.4}
\]

Combining (CP.1)--(CP.2), the number `V_simp,15=S_15-A_15` of simple-outer straight
vertices satisfies

\[
\boxed{
{V_{simp,15}\over D}
\ge
1.1083751522061389353\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{CP.5}
\]

Every one of these vertices has the bounded-face local classification from the
phase-gate theorem:

- degree three: exceptional-face incidence, mixed two-rhombus transmission gate, or
  double-obtuse two-rhombus gate;
- degree four: exceptional-face incidence or three-rhombus acute fan.

The previously explicit articulation escape is therefore quantitatively minor at the
current endpoint: it can absorb at most about `3.98%` of the `15`-degree straight
vertex population.

---

## 3. Signed inward direction for every extra contact

Use the orientation convention of the signed penetration theorem. For a straight
occurrence on `W_i`, let `n_i` be the loaded-hull side normal pointing into the convex
force hull.

The signed local argument applies to **every** extra contact at the straight vertex,
not just to a chosen system-of-distinct-representatives edge. At `beta=pi/12`, every
extra contact `xw` obeys

\[
\boxed{
(w-x)\cdot n_i
>\sin(\pi/4)
={1\over\sqrt2}.
}
\tag{CP.6}
\]

At a cubic straight vertex there is exactly one such extra incidence; at a quartic
straight vertex there are exactly two.

Let `V_3,V_4` be the degree-three and degree-four straight-vertex counts. Then

\[
V_3+V_4=S_{15}.
\tag{CP.7}
\]

The total number of extra incidences is

\[
\boxed{I_{all}=V_3+2V_4=S_{15}+V_4.}
\tag{CP.8}
\]

---

## 4. All extra incidences are globally edge-injective

We adapt the already hostile-audited signed-injectivity argument, with its stronger
`15`-degree threshold.

Suppose one physical unit contact `xy` were an extra edge at straight occurrences at
both endpoints. Let the associated loaded-hull inward normals be `n_i,n_j`, and put

\[
e=y-x,
\qquad |e|=1.
\]

Equation (CP.6) at `x` gives

\[
e\cdot n_i>{1\over\sqrt2},
\]

while at `y` the selected direction is `-e`, so

\[
e\cdot n_j<-{1\over\sqrt2}.
\]

Hence `n_i` lies within angle `<pi/4` of `e` and `n_j` lies within angle `<pi/4` of
`-e`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{\angle(n_i,n_j)>\pi/2.}
\tag{CP.9}
\]

Let `M_i,M_j` be the midpoints of the associated loaded-hull sides. Type-II near-disk
geometry makes their radial directions `o(1)`-aligned with the side normals and their
radii `D/2+o(D)`. Thus (CP.9) yields

\[
\boxed{|M_i-M_j|>{D\over\sqrt2}-o(D).}
\tag{CP.10}
\]

For the route detours `d_i,d_j`, the same route-midpoint estimate used in the signed
penetration theorem gives

\[
|M_i-M_j|
\le s_i+s_j+1+{d_i+d_j\over2},
\tag{CP.11}
\]

where the largest loaded-hull side satisfies `max s_r=o(D)`. Therefore

\[
\boxed{d_i+d_j>\sqrt2D-o(D).}
\tag{CP.12}
\]

This is impossible because

\[
d_i+d_j\le d_{out}\le0.002883975D+o(D).
\]

So no physical edge can appear as an extra contact incidence at two straight
endpoints. Distinct extra incidences at one endpoint are already distinct physical
edges. Therefore the incidence-to-edge map is globally injective:

\[
\boxed{E_{all,15}=I_{all}=V_3+2V_4=S_{15}+V_4.}
\tag{CP.13}
\]

In particular

\[
\boxed{
{E_{all,15}\over D}
\ge1.1543116921171727469\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{CP.14}
\]

and every counted edge has signed inward component greater than `1/sqrt(2)` relative
to its associated loaded-hull side.

The exact degree/edge identity is

\[
\boxed{E_{all,15}+V_3=2S_{15}.}
\tag{CP.15}
\]

Thus, for example,

\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{either}&V_3/D\ge0.57715584605858637\ldots-o(1),\\[1mm]
\text{or}&E_{all,15}/D\ge1.73146753817575912\ldots-o(1).
\end{array}
}
\tag{CP.16}
\]

The second branch is a very large population of distinct `>45`-degree signed inward
penetration contacts.

---

## 5. Strengthened phase-gate normal form

Retain the phase-gate theorem's notation:

- `X` = simple-outer straight vertices incident to a bounded exceptional face;
- `O` = cubic simple-outer double-obtuse gates;
- `P` = simple-outer all-rhombic transmission gates;
- `Delta_train` = total strict-rhombus angular defect;
- `f,Q` = bounded exceptional-face count and degree-above-four excess.

The local incidence and defect charges are

\[
X\le4f+Q,
\tag{CP.17}
\]

\[
O<{24\over\pi}\Delta_{train}.
\tag{CP.18}
\]

Since the repeated-outer population has already been removed in (CP.5),

\[
\boxed{
P
\ge
V_{simp,15}-(4f+Q)-{24\over\pi}\Delta_{train}.
}
\tag{CP.19}
\]

At the current endpoint,

\[
\boxed{
{P\over D}
\ge
1.1083751522061389353\ldots
-{4f+Q\over D}
-{24\over\pi}{\Delta_{train}\over D}
-o(1).
}
\tag{CP.20}
\]

After the canonical Section-68 phase-circle cut, at most
`12 Delta_train/pi` additional gate vertices can be hit by a deleted rhombus relation.
Therefore the fully retained transmission-gate count satisfies

\[
\boxed{
{P_{ret}\over D}
\ge
1.1083751522061389353\ldots
-{4f+Q\over D}
-{36\over\pi}{\Delta_{train}\over D}
-o(1).
}
\tag{CP.21}
\]

This is the first composition in the current stack that simultaneously has

1. a branch-independent `>1.1D` simple physical boundary-vertex floor;
2. exact local degree `3/4` classification;
3. globally distinct strongly signed inward extra contacts; and
4. an explicit payment formula into exceptional-face incidence, angular defect, or
   retained train-phase transmission gates.

---

## 6. Remaining closure target

C-II is still open. The remaining statement is now an occupancy theorem, not a local
geometry theorem.

If `(4f+Q)` pays most of (CP.21), then a linear fraction of the near-simple outer
boundary is incident to exceptional faces. The low-`t` topology theorem says those
faces are overwhelmingly pentagonal and already carry linear closure-rank / stopped-
train obligations. The missing step is to show that the same pentagon network cannot
also absorb the signed straight penetrations at the required incidence density.

If the angular-defect term pays most of (CP.21), then the global punctured-packing
bound must be near its boundary-order ceiling. That is a quantitatively rigid branch
which should be coupled to the near-isodiametric hull rather than treated as free
`O(D)` slack.

If neither currency pays, (CP.21) leaves a linear family of retained phase-transmission
gates. Those gates lie in the exact real phase-potential network after the Section-68
cut. A closing theorem can target stopped-train occupancy or a simultaneous coherent
shear.

No individual KKT coefficient floor or individual rhombus-defect floor is used.

**LEVEL I STATUS: STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**
